WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.429
Identity is one of the most important design choices in an MCP system.

2
00:00:04.429 --> 00:00:13.593
A user may ask for an action, the AI host may decide how to assist, an MCP server may expose the tool, and a downstream API may enforce permissions.

3
00:00:13.593 --> 00:00:17.564
Those layers should not be collapsed into one vague identity.

4
00:00:17.564 --> 00:00:21.917
User authorization is different from application authorization.

5
00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:25.735
A service account is different from a delegated user token.

6
00:00:25.735 --> 00:00:30.088
A model-generated request is different from verified user intent.

7
00:00:30.088 --> 00:00:35.433
Delegated access lets an application act on behalf of a user within defined limits.

8
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:44.826
OAuth-style concepts such as scopes, audience, token lifetime, and consent are useful mental models even when the exact implementation varies.

9
00:00:44.826 --> 00:00:48.034
Scope should describe what an integration can do.

10
00:00:48.034 --> 00:00:51.394
Audience should constrain where a token can be used.

11
00:00:51.394 --> 00:00:56.205
Consent should make the user or administrator aware of meaningful access.

12
00:00:56.205 --> 00:01:00.710
Token lifetime should limit exposure if credentials are mishandled.

13
00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:04.299
Careless token passthrough can blur trust boundaries.

14
00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:17.281
If a powerful user token is passed through multiple components, tools, or servers without clear policy, components may gain access they do not need and users may not understand what is being authorized.

15
00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:32.249
Safer designs separate user intent from model-generated action requests, issue narrowly scoped credentials where possible, keep secrets out of prompts and logs, and enforce authorization at the application or service boundary.

16
00:01:32.249 --> 00:01:36.984
MCP tools should not gain permission merely because a model asks for them.

17
00:01:36.984 --> 00:01:42.482
The model can propose an action, but policy must decide whether the action is allowed.

18
00:01:42.482 --> 00:01:46.835
High-impact actions should require explicit consent or approval.

19
00:01:46.835 --> 00:01:55.999
Permission checks should consider the user, the tool, the resource, the requested operation, the data sensitivity, and the current context.

20
00:01:55.999 --> 00:02:02.642
Consent is not a decorative prompt; it is a control that helps connect user intent to authorized action.
